        <body>
  <h1>Student Rights & Discipline in Newburgh Enlarged City SD: A Parent's Guide</h1>

  <p>At Newburgh Enlarged City School District, located in Newburgh, Orange County, we aim to provide a safe and supportive learning environment for all students. This commitment includes upholding student rights and ensuring fair, consistent, and legally compliant disciplinary procedures, especially for students with disabilities. This guide provides expert-level information about discipline, Manifestation Determination Reviews (MDRs), Due Process rights, and advocacy strategies specific to Newburgh Enlarged City SD.</p>

  <h2>Understanding Student Discipline in New York State and Newburgh ECSD</h2>

  <p>New York State Education Law and the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) provide significant protections for students facing disciplinary action, particularly those with IEPs or 504 plans.  Discipline in Newburgh ECSD must align with these legal frameworks. It’s crucial to understand the difference between short-term suspensions (5 days or less) and long-term suspensions (more than 10 days), as the procedures and rights differ substantially. Newburgh ECSD's Code of Conduct should be readily available on the district's website. Familiarize yourself with it to understand specific prohibited behaviors and corresponding disciplinary actions.</p>

  <h2>Manifestation Determination Review (MDR): Protecting Students with Disabilities</h2>

  <p>A key aspect of disciplinary procedures, especially for students with IEPs or 504 plans, is the Manifestation Determination Review (MDR).  According to IDEA (20 U.S.C. § 1415(k)(1)(E)), an MDR is required when a student with a disability faces a suspension exceeding 10 consecutive school days or a pattern of removals that constitute a change of placement (more than 10 cumulative days in a school year where the behavior is substantially similar).</p>

  <p><b>What Happens During an MDR?</b> The MDR team, including parents/guardians, relevant school personnel (including the IEP team), and potentially the student, convenes to determine if the student's behavior was:
    <ul>
      <li>Caused by, or directly and substantially related to, the student's disability; <b>OR</b></li>
      <li>The direct result of the school district's failure to implement the IEP or 504 plan.</li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <p><b>Key Participants in the MDR:</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Parents/Guardians:</b> Your active participation is crucial. Bring documentation, observations, and any relevant information about your child's disability and behavior.</li>
    <li><b>School Psychologist/Special Education Teacher:</b>  They provide expertise on the student's disability and the IEP/504 plan.</li>
    <li><b>General Education Teacher(s):</b> Input on the student's behavior in the general education setting.</li>
    <li><b>School Administrator:</b>  Ensures the MDR process is followed correctly.</li>
    <li><b>Student (if appropriate):</b>  Depending on age and maturity, the student's perspective can be valuable.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><b>If the Behavior is a Manifestation:</b></p>
  <p>If the MDR team determines the behavior *is* a manifestation of the disability, the IEP team *must* conduct a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA), unless one has already been conducted. They *must* also create or revise a Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP) to address the behavior.  (34 CFR § 300.530(f)). The student then returns to their current placement, unless the parents and the school agree to a change of placement as part of the IEP development.</p>

  <p><b>If the Behavior is NOT a Manifestation:</b></p>
  <p>If the MDR team determines the behavior *is not* a manifestation of the disability, the student can be disciplined in the same manner as students without disabilities.  However, the school district is still required to provide Free Appropriate Public Education (FAPE) to the student during the period of removal.  This may involve alternative educational settings or services to ensure the student continues to make progress on their IEP goals.  It's vital to scrutinize this determination. Challenge assumptions, present your own evidence, and consider seeking an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) if you disagree.</p>

  <p><b>Common Pitfalls to Watch Out For:</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Pre-determination:</b> Ensure the school hasn't already decided the outcome before the MDR meeting.  Listen for statements suggesting the decision is already made.</li>
    <li><b>Inadequate IEP Implementation:</b>  Scrutinize whether the IEP was followed correctly *before* the incident.  Was the student receiving all accommodations and modifications? Were BIP strategies implemented consistently?</li>
    <li><b>Lack of Data:</b>  Demand data to support the school's claims about the behavior.  Vague statements are insufficient.</li>
    <li><b>Ignoring Parent Input:</b>  Your perspective is critical.  Ensure your concerns are heard and addressed.</li>
    <li><b>Insufficient Time:</b>  The MDR meeting needs adequate time for thorough discussion.  Don't be rushed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Suspensions in Newburgh Enlarged City SD: Your Rights and Responsibilities</h2>

  <p>If your child faces a suspension, understanding your rights is paramount.  New York State Education Law § 3214 outlines the procedures for student suspensions. You have the right to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Written Notification:</b>  Receive written notification of the reasons for the suspension, including specific details about the alleged misconduct. This notice should be provided in your native language (if applicable) and should be timely.</li>
      <li><b>Informal Conference:</b>  An opportunity to discuss the charges with the school principal or other designated school official *before* a short-term suspension (5 days or less). This is your chance to present your child's side of the story and any mitigating circumstances.</li>
      <li><b>Formal Hearing:</b>  A formal hearing for suspensions exceeding five school days. You have the right to representation by an attorney or advocate, to present evidence and witnesses, and to cross-examine school witnesses.  This hearing must be conducted impartially.</li>
      <li><b>Appeal:</b>  The right to appeal the suspension decision to the Superintendent of Schools (or their designee) and, subsequently, to the Board of Education.</li>
      <li><b>Translation/Interpretation Services:</b>  The right to receive all communication and participate in all meetings with the aid of a translator or interpreter, if needed.</li>
    </ul>

  <p><b>Step-by-Step Guide to Responding to a Suspension:</b></p>
  <ol>
    <li><b>Review the Suspension Notice:</b> Carefully read the notice to understand the charges against your child.  Note the date, time, and location of the alleged incident.</li>
    <li><b>Gather Information:</b>  Talk to your child about the incident.  Gather any relevant information, such as witness statements or documentation.</li>
    <li><b>Attend the Informal Conference (if applicable):</b>  Prepare to present your child's side of the story calmly and rationally.  Bring any supporting documentation.</li>
    <li><b>Request a Formal Hearing (for suspensions over 5 days):</b>  Make this request in writing and within the timeframe specified in the suspension notice.</li>
    <li><b>Prepare for the Hearing:</b>  Gather evidence, identify witnesses, and consider seeking legal representation or advocacy support.</li>
    <li><b>Attend the Hearing:</b>  Present your case clearly and concisely.  Cross-examine school witnesses effectively.</li>
    <li><b>Appeal the Decision (if necessary):</b>  Follow the district's appeal process and adhere to deadlines.</li>
    <li><b>Document Everything:</b>  Keep copies of all correspondence, notices, and hearing transcripts.</li>
  </ol>

  <p><b>Advocacy Tips for Suspension Hearings:</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Focus on the Facts:</b>  Present a clear and concise account of the events.</li>
    <li><b>Highlight Mitigating Circumstances:</b>  Explain any factors that may have contributed to the behavior, such as stress, bullying, or a misunderstanding.</li>
    <li><b>Emphasize the Student's Strengths:</b>  Remind the school of your child's positive qualities and contributions to the school community.</li>
    <li><b>Challenge the Evidence:</b>  If the school's evidence is weak or unreliable, point out the inconsistencies.</li>
    <li><b>Advocate for Alternatives to Suspension:</b>  Suggest alternative disciplinary measures, such as restorative justice practices or counseling.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Due Process Rights Under IDEA</h2>

  <p>If you disagree with the school district's decisions regarding your child's special education needs (including disciplinary actions), you have the right to due process under IDEA (20 U.S.C. § 1415).  Due process is a formal legal process that allows you to resolve disputes with the school district.</p>

  <p><b>Steps in the Due Process Process:</b></p>
  <ol>
    <li><b>Filing a Due Process Complaint:</b>  You must file a written complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED) and provide a copy to the school district. The complaint must include specific information about the issues in dispute, the facts supporting your position, and the relief you are seeking.</li>
    <li><b>Resolution Session:</b>  Within 15 days of receiving the due process complaint, the school district must convene a resolution session with you and relevant members of the IEP team. The purpose of the resolution session is to attempt to resolve the dispute without going to a hearing.</li>
    <li><b>Due Process Hearing:</b>  If the dispute is not resolved during the resolution session, a due process hearing will be conducted by an impartial hearing officer (IHO) appointed by NYSED.  You have the right to present evidence, call witnesses, and cross-examine the school district's witnesses.</li>
    <li><b>Hearing Officer Decision:</b>  The IHO will issue a written decision within 45 days of the end of the hearing. The decision is binding on both you and the school district, unless it is appealed.</li>
    <li><b>Appeal:</b>  You can appeal the IHO's decision to a State Review Officer (SRO) at NYSED. The SRO will review the record of the due process hearing and issue a final decision.</li>
    <li><b>Civil Action:</b>  If you disagree with the SRO's decision, you can file a civil action in state or federal court.</li>
  </ol>

  <p><b>Important Considerations for Due Process:</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Statute of Limitations:</b>  There is a statute of limitations for filing a due process complaint, so it's important to act quickly. In New York, you typically have two years from the date you knew or should have known about the alleged violation to file a complaint (though this can vary based on the specific circumstances).</li>
    <li><b>Legal Representation:</b>  Due process hearings can be complex and adversarial.  Consider seeking legal representation from a special education attorney or advocate.</li>
    <li><b>"Stay Put" Provision:</b>  During the due process proceedings, your child has the right to remain in their current educational placement (the "stay put" provision), unless you and the school district agree otherwise.  This is a crucial protection that prevents the school from unilaterally changing your child's placement while the dispute is pending.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>

  <details>
    <summary><b>What is the difference between a suspension and an expulsion?</b></summary>
    <p>A suspension is a temporary removal from school, while an expulsion is a permanent removal. Expulsions are rare in New York State, particularly for students with disabilities.</p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary><b>Can my child be suspended for behavior that occurs off-campus?</b></summary>
    <p>Yes, under certain circumstances. According to New York State Education Law § 3214, a student can be suspended for off-campus behavior if it directly affects the safety, health, or well-being of students or staff within the school setting.</p>
  </details>

    <details>
    <summary><b>What if my child has an un-diagnosed disability but is displaying behaviors that suggest a disability?</b></summary>
    <p>If the school district has a basis of knowledge that your child may have a disability *before* the behavior that led to disciplinary action, they are required to provide certain protections under IDEA (34 CFR § 300.534). A "basis of knowledge" exists if:
      <ul>
        <li>The parent expressed concern in writing to supervisory or administrative personnel of the agency that the child is in need of special education and related services;</li>
        <li>The behavior or performance of the child demonstrates the need for such services;</li>
        <li>The parent of the child has requested an evaluation.</li>
      </ul>
      If a basis of knowledge exists, the school district must conduct an expedited evaluation to determine if the child has a disability. In the meantime, the student is entitled to interim protections.
       </p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary><b>Where can I find the Newburgh Enlarged City SD Code of Conduct?</b></summary>
    <p>The Newburgh Enlarged City School District Code of Conduct should be available on the district's website (www.newburghschools.org). You can also request a copy from your child's school or the district's administration office.</p>
  </details>

  <p><i>Disclaimer: This information is for general guidance only and does not constitute legal advice. Consult with a qualified special education attorney or advocate for specific legal advice regarding your situation.</i></p>
</body>
